    <h1>Fast Powers</h1>

    <p>This primarily serves as a test post, but I wanted to write something about a fast power function for unsigned integers that runs in $O(\log n)$.</p>
    
    <p>The motivation for why I crossed paths with this problem was because I wanted a fast power function for computing the spectral brdf for rasterization. 
    Since I am using C++ and std::pow is slow (not really, g++ will probably optimize it) I thought that writing a fast power function would be a fun challenge. </p>
    
    <p>Since this function needs to be quick, recursion is out of the window. So we need some loop to do this quickly, but how? Well, let's begin with an example. 
    \[
    x^{21}
    \]
    this is equal to 
    \[
    x^{21} = x^{16} \cdot x^4 \cdot x 
    \]
    notice the binary rep of $\mathtt{21}_{10}$ is $\mathtt{10101}_{2}$ and each 1 corresponds to which power of 2 we need. To be quick we should store the last
    highest element we made. The program should also use $\mathtt{>>1}$ to find the next $\mathtt{1}_{2}$ in pow and like that you have a fast power function!
    </p>

  <p>
  This is actually a well-known idea and a common implementation looks like: 
  </p>
    
<pre><code style="font-family: monospace; background: #f4f4f4; display: block; padding: 12px; border-radius: 4px;">
double fastPow(double val, unsigned int exp) {
    double res = 1.0;
    while (exp > 0) {
        if (exp & 1) {
            res *= val;
        }
        val *= val;
        exp >>= 1;
    }
    return res;
}
</code></pre>

<p>
A footnote about this function is that pow must be an unsigned int, because of two's complement if -1 is inputted the program experiences biblical levels of overflow. 
You can handle this edge case with a check for negative exponent without much loss in numbers we can represent, but where is the fun in that?
</p>

